Q: Extend signal form&#39;s FormField directive A: <p>In Angular we have two approach to &quot;enchance a directive&quot;.</p>
<p>1.-Extends your new directive from another one</p>
<pre><code>@Directive({
  selector: '[axFormField]',
})
export class AxFormFieldDirective&lt;T&gt; extends FormField&lt;T&gt; {..}

//And you use 
&lt;input [axFormField]=&quot;form.name&quot;/&gt;

</code></pre>
<p>2.-Extends the &quot;properties&quot; of the tag.</p>
<pre><code>//see that you not use &quot;providers&quot;
@Directive({
  //the selector can be simply [axForField], but if you
  //use [formField][axFormField] you be sure it's apply only if have a formField
  selector: '[formField][axFormField]'
})
export class AxFormFieldDirective{
  formfield=inject(FormField)
}
//And you sue
&lt;input axFormField [formField]=&quot;form.name&quot;/&gt;

</code></pre>
<p>Since FormField is experimental (in Angular 21.0.0 is exporteAs <code>field</code>, in Angular 21.1.0 is exportedAs <code>formField</code>, in Angular 22.0.0 change again (really it's a good job, but it's crazy so many changes) the best bet is use the second approach.</p>
<p>BTW, as Mathie says, since Angular 21.0.0 you can use</p>
<pre><code>bootstrapApplication(App,{
  providers: [
    provideSignalFormsConfig({
      classes: NG_STATUS_CLASSES,
    }),
  ]});

</code></pre>
<p>And Angular add the class ng-valid,ng-invalud, ng-touched,...</p>
<p>or use customs class,e.g.</p>
<pre><code>bootstrapApplication(App,{
  providers: [
    provideSignalFormsConfig({
      classes: {
  'touched': ({ state }) =&gt; state.touched(),
  'untouched': ({ state }) =&gt; !state.touched(),
  'dirty': ({ state }) =&gt; state.dirty(),
  'pristine': ({ state }) =&gt; !state.dirty(),
  'valid': ({ state }) =&gt; state.valid(),
  'invalid': ({ state }) =&gt; state.invalid(),
  'pending': ({ state }) =&gt; state.pending(),
};
    }),
  ]});


</code></pre>

Q: Invoking tar.exe from PowerShell, can&#39;t input any more commands A: <p>There seem to be a confusion, your file isn't a tarball, it'd have the <code>.tar.gz</code> or <code>.tgz</code> extension otherwise; I don't know if <code>tar.exe</code> is able to handle <strong>single gzip compressed files</strong>.</p>
<p>So as an alternative that does work, you can use the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.io.compression.gzipstream" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>GZipStream</code> class</a> to expand it:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$uri = 'https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc-4.6.4/libstdc++/libstdc++-manual.pdf.gz'
# this should be changed for the destination of the expanded archive
# currently it will be a file with this name to the current location
$path = Join-Path $pwd libstdc++-manual.pdf

try {
    $req = Invoke-WebRequest $uri -UseBasicParsing -EA 1
    $gz = [System.IO.Compression.GZipStream]::new(
        $req.RawContentStream,
        [System.IO.Compression.CompressionMode]::Decompress)

    $fs = [System.IO.File]::OpenWrite($path)
    $gz.CopyTo($fs)
}
finally {
    if ($gz) { $gz.Dispose() }
    if ($fs) { $fs.Dispose() }
}

Invoke-Item $path # Should open the pdf
</code></pre>

